  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "What is the center and the radius of the circle with equation $(x-5)^{2}+{y}^{2}=3?$",
  "worked_solution": "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's compare the general equation to our equation side-by-side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-5)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =3 \\\\ (x-5)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} & ={(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $a=5,$ $b=0,$ and $r=\\sqrt{3}.$ \n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(5,0)$ and its radius is $\\sqrt{3}.$",
